---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'

const config = `import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test'

export default defineConfig({
  reporter: [
    ['line'],
    ['suitecut/reporter', { outputFile: '.suitecut/latest-run.json' }],
  ],
  use: { trace: 'on', video: 'off' },
})`
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install and configure SuiteCut for a Playwright project."
  section="overview"
>
  <p class="kicker">Field manual · 01</p>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p>
    SuiteCut records a Playwright run into a strict manifest, then renders that saved material into
    MP4 or WebM. Test execution and video rendering stay separate.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Node.js 22 or newer and Playwright 1.59 or newer.</li>
    <li>Chromium installed through Playwright.</li>
    <li>FFmpeg and FFprobe available on <code>PATH</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="npm install --save-dev suitecut @playwright/test" />
  <p>On macOS, install the media tools with <code>brew install ffmpeg</code>.</p>

  <h2>Configure Playwright</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={config} />
  <div class="note">
    Keep Playwright video off. SuiteCut owns the screencast so it can align every page with its own
    narration, overlays, and timeline.
  </div>

  <h2>Record and render</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    label="Terminal"
    code={`suitecut test --manifest .suitecut/latest-run.json -- tests/tour.spec.ts

suitecut render \\
  --manifest .suitecut/latest-run.json \\
  --output .suitecut/videos/tour.mp4`}
  />
  <p>
    The renderer writes a report beside the video with its selected attempt, diagnostics, process
    result, and output duration.
  </p>

  <h2>Render 4K at 60 fps</h2>
  <CodeBlock
    label="Terminal"
    code={`suitecut render \\
  --manifest .suitecut/latest-run.json \\
  --output .suitecut/videos/tour-4k.mp4 \\
  --width 3840 \\
  --height 2160 \\
  --fps 60`}
  />
  <p>
    SuiteCut captures the physical viewport size by default. Playwright will downscale a requested
    screencast size, but it will not upscale beyond the CSS viewport. Set the Playwright viewport to
    the required native source dimensions when native 4K capture matters.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
